fpcmoh: do not let a failed cleanup transfer sink a completed operation

FP_VERIFY_CANCEL is the verify/identify SSM's start_cleanup state, so
libfprint runs it at the end of *every* verify and identify -- on success as
well as on failure. fpi_ssm_mark_completed() does not finish an SSM that is
still below start_cleanup; it jumps to the cleanup state first:

    /* libfprint/fpi-ssm.c, fpi_ssm_mark_completed */
    /* complete in a cleanup state just moves forward one step */
    if (machine->cur_state < machine->start_cleanup)
      next_state = machine->start_cleanup;

So FP_VERIFY_IDENTIFY (14), which reports the match result and calls
fpi_ssm_mark_completed(), is always followed by FP_VERIFY_CANCEL (17).

The cleanup state passed fpi_ssm_usb_transfer_cb as its completion handler,
and that helper calls fpi_ssm_mark_failed() whenever the transfer errors. A
cleanup command the device declines to answer therefore turns an operation
that has already succeeded into a failed one.

On FPC 10a5:a900 (Honor MagicBook, firmware 22.26.2.29) this is reliably
fatal. The firmware acknowledges bRequest 0x03 during init in about 2 ms, but
never answers it once a capture has completed; the transfer times out after
1 s. Every enroll fails with "Device reported an error during identify for
enroll: transfer timed out", after the image was captured and the host-side
matcher had already run and reported correctly. It is not wValue-specific --
0x0000 and 0x0001 were both tested and behave identically.

Use a cleanup-specific callback that logs the failure and completes the state
instead of failing the machine.

This cannot mask a real error. fpi_ssm_mark_failed keeps only the first error
recorded on a machine:

    if (!machine->error)
      machine->error = g_steal_pointer (&error);
    else
      g_error_free (error);

so a failure raised in states 0..14 is already set on the SSM before cleanup
runs, and completing cleanup without adding a new error leaves it intact for
fpc_verify_ssm_done() to report via fpi_ssm_get_error(). The change is
strictly a narrowing of when this SSM reports failure.

This is not specific to the a900: any FPC MoH device whose firmware does not
answer 0x03 in this state loses every operation the same way.

Applies on top of merge request 396.
---
--- a/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
@@ -825,6 +825,49 @@
   return result;
 }
 
+/* Completion handler for transfers issued from a *cleanup* state.
+ *
+ * FP_VERIFY_CANCEL is this SSM's start_cleanup state, so libfprint runs it at
+ * the end of every verify/identify -- on success as well as on failure.
+ * fpi_ssm_mark_completed() does not finish an SSM that is still below
+ * start_cleanup; it jumps to the cleanup state first (fpi-ssm.c: "complete in
+ * a cleanup state just moves forward one step"). A successful identify
+ * therefore reaches FP_VERIFY_CANCEL just like a failed one does.
+ *
+ * That makes fpi_ssm_usb_transfer_cb the wrong callback here: it calls
+ * fpi_ssm_mark_failed() on transfer error, so a cleanup command the device
+ * declines to answer turns an operation that already succeeded into a failure.
+ *
+ * Observed on FPC 10a5:a900 (Honor MagicBook, FW 22.26.2.29): the firmware
+ * acknowledges bRequest 0x03 during init in ~2 ms, but never answers it once a
+ * capture has completed. The transfer times out after 1 s and sinks an
+ * identify whose result was already reported. Not wValue-specific -- both
+ * 0x0000 and 0x0001 were tested and behave the same.
+ *
+ * Swallowing the error here cannot mask a genuine failure: fpi_ssm_mark_failed
+ * keeps only the *first* error ("if (!machine->error) machine->error = ..."),
+ * so an error raised in states 0..14 is already recorded on the SSM by the time
+ * cleanup runs, and completing cleanup without adding a new one leaves it
+ * intact for fpc_verify_ssm_done() to report.
+ */
+static void
+fpc_verify_cleanup_cb (FpiUsbTransfer *transfer,
+                       FpDevice       *device,
+                       gpointer        user_data,
+                       GError         *error)
+{
+  g_return_if_fail (transfer->ssm);
+
+  if (error)
+    {
+      fp_dbg ("%s: cleanup transfer failed (%s); not failing the operation",
+              G_STRFUNC, error->message);
+      g_clear_error (&error);
+    }
+
+  fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);
+}
+
 static void
 fpc_verify_suspend_resume_cb (FpiUsbTransfer *transfer,
                               FpDevice       *device,
@@ -944,7 +987,9 @@
 
     case FP_VERIFY_CANCEL:
       {
-        fpc_write_ctrl (ssm, device, 0x03, 0x01, NULL, 0, fpi_ssm_usb_transfer_cb, NULL);
+        /* Cleanup state -- see fpc_verify_cleanup_cb. A device that declines
+         * to answer this must not sink an operation that already succeeded. */
+        fpc_write_ctrl (ssm, device, 0x03, 0x01, NULL, 0, fpc_verify_cleanup_cb, NULL);
       }
       break;
     }
